> That being said I'd be surprised that it is the cause. A process is a zombie when the parent fails to [wait(2)](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/wait.2.html). And it is more likely that said process is Gitea itself.

How exactly do you think it could happen that Gitea doesn't wait?

https://github.com/go-gitea/gitea/blob/d002e3da008ec6945a405e4f14941bd42a341494/modules/git/command.go#L164-L177

The only way that something doesn't wait() here is if there is a panic in PipelineFunc but there's no PipelineFunc here and the panic would be logged.

<details><summary>Looking deeper at cmd.Wait()</summary>

From os/exec/exec.go:

```go
func (c *Cmd) Wait() error {
	if c.Process == nil {
		return errors.New("exec: not started")
	}
	if c.finished {
		return errors.New("exec: Wait was already called")
	}
	c.finished = true

	state, err := c.Process.Wait()
	if c.waitDone != nil {
		close(c.waitDone)
	}
	c.ProcessState = state

	var copyError error
	for range c.goroutine {
		if err := <-c.errch; err != nil && copyError == nil {
			copyError = err
		}
	}

	c.closeDescriptors(c.closeAfterWait)

	if err != nil {
		return err
	} else if !state.Success() {
		return &ExitError{ProcessState: state}
	}

	return copyError
}

...

// Wait waits for the Process to exit, and then returns a
// ProcessState describing its status and an error, if any.
// Wait releases any resources associated with the Process.
// On most operating systems, the Process must be a child
